      "process_notes": {
        "timebox_status": "EXCEEDED substantially. Brief allotted 2 hours; this probe ran roughly 8 hours wall-clock across two files (most of it unattended background transfer time, not active analysis -- but still far past the nominal timebox).",
        "network_issue": "HuggingFace-backed storage for this dataset throttled to ~1-2.4 MB/s regardless of approach (single stream, 8-way parallel range requests, or pandas' own urllib-based URL streaming -- the last of these additionally exhibited a separate near-total stall unrelated to bandwidth, cause not fully diagnosed, abandoned in favor of a working approach rather than debugged further under time pressure).",
        "tooling_bugs_found_and_fixed": [
          "All_external.csv: first working attempt crashed at 75% on a ParserError from manually splitting the byte buffer on the last newline, which occasionally landed inside a quoted multi-line field. Fixed by feeding the httpx byte stream through a file-like adapter directly into pandas' own C-engine tokenizer (verified against a synthetic adversarial test before rerunning).",
          "nasdaq_exteral_data.csv: crashed at 73% on a pandas-internal IndexError in _concatenate_chunks, triggered by combining usecols with chunked low_memory dtype-consistency checks. Fixed with dtype=str + low_memory=False. Recovered via a resumed run from an estimated byte offset (1GB safety margin) rather than a full 3hr restart; 642 duplicate rows near the resume boundary removed afterward."
        ],
        "alternatives_not_evaluated": "Non-FNSPID corpora (GDELT, live yfinance/RSS headlines) were not evaluated, per the brief's instructions for a source that proves unworkable. FNSPID is not unworkable in the sense of 'unobtainable' -- both files were fully retrieved and analyzed -- but the granularity finding above means the spike's core assumption (testable same-bar leakage) cannot be demonstrated against this corpus as originally scoped. This is the decision point for Phase 1."
      }
